[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,4.0);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.4,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.8,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.45*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,2.0) {Distance from driveway top};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Theo starts skateboarding down his driveway, gradually building up speed. The graph shows his distance from the top of the driveway. Which statement correctly describes his motion?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.4);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.0,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.3*(\x-3.0)*(\x-3.0)+2.7});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.7) {Distance from tent};
\node[font=\small] at (1.8,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Layla walks away from her tent along a track, quickly at first and then more gradually. The graph shows her distance from the tent. Which statement correctly describes her motion?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.2);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.0,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2.7-0.3*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.6) {Distance from lodge};
\node[font=\small] at (1.8,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Marco skis back down towards the lodge after reaching the top of the run. The graph shows his distance from the lodge. Which statement correctly describes his motion?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.6);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.8,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{3.2*exp(-0.85*\x)});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.8) {Distance from owner};
\node[font=\small] at (1.9,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A dog runs back towards its owner, fast at first and then slowing as it gets close. The graph shows the dog's distance from its owner. Which statement correctly describes its motion?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.05]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.0);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.4,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (1.4,1.4);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.4,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+1.4},{1.4+0.4*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.5) {Distance travelled};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Grace jogs at an even pace, then sprints, building up further speed. The graph shows her distance travelled. Which statement correctly describes her motion?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.6);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.7,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.1,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.32*(\x-3.1)*(\x-3.1)+3.075});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.8) {Distance from home};
\node[font=\small] at (1.85,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Ben cycles away from home. The graph shows his distance from home. Which statement correctly describes his motion?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,2.4);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (5.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (1.4,1.4);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.4,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+1.4},{1.4+(-0.32*(\x-1.4)*(\x-1.4)+0.627)});
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.6,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+2.8},{2.027-0.3*\x*\x});
\draw[thick,blue] (4.4,1.259) -- (5.0,1.259);
\node[font=\small] at (0.7,0.55) {A};
\node[font=\small] at (2.1,1.95) {B};
\node[font=\small] at (3.6,1.85) {C};
\node[font=\small] at (4.7,1.45) {D};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.2) {Distance from home};
\node[font=\small] at (2.8,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph shows a walker's distance from home over four labelled stages. During which stage is the walker speeding up?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\begin{scope}[xshift=0cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.8);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.45*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.4) {Distance};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.3,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.3,-1.1) {Graph A};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=4.0cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.8);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.22*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.4) {Distance};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.3,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.3,-1.1) {Graph B};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Ana and Ben each start from rest and speed up along straight paths. Graph A shows Ana's distance from the start; Graph B shows Ben's distance from the start, over the same length of time. Which of them speeds up more quickly?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.15]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,2.4);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.8,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.28*(\x-1.8)*(\x-1.8)+0.907});
\draw[thick,blue] (1.8,0.907) -- (1.2,0.6);
\draw[thick,blue] (1.2,0.6) -- (3.2,2.1);
\node[font=\small] at (0.6,0.45) {A};
\node[font=\small] at (1.55,0.9) {B};
\node[font=\small] at (2.5,1.6) {C};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.2) {Distance from home};
\node[font=\small] at (1.8,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Priya sketched this graph of her walk to the shops, labelling three parts A, B and C, but it cannot show a real journey. Which labelled part could not represent a real journey?
